WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.570
Hi my name is Jon Harmon.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:11.090
I run the R4DS
online learning community.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:14.420
Our community operates
primarily through a Slack team

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.390
and we invite R learners
at all skill levels

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.460
to join us at r4ds.io/join

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.430
Among other things,
we host book clubs

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.410
for R related books.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.800
Whenever we start a new cohort
for one of the book clubs,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.200
we need to find a time
that works for as many

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.730
of the people interested in
reading the book as we can.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.630
We used to use a generic
free online app for everyone

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:40.980
to sign up.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.930
Everyone would
type in their name,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.480
choose their available
times and days,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.880
and then set a password
in case they wanted

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.950
to edit their selections.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.050
Sometimes people
would sign up twice,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.280
not remembering the John who
was signed up already was them.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.100
At least one time someone
had a typo in their name,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.890
so when they came back to
change their available hours,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.810
it counted as a new person.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.150
And of course, sometimes
we legitimately

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.830
had multiple people with the
same name of signing up.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.810
So I didn't have any way to sort out
who was who and how many duplicates

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:13.920
we had.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.470
And even when everything
went smoothly,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.630
I didn't have an
easy way to connect

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.170
the information in that
app to their Slack user.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.240
So, I couldn't contact
people directly

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.280
to let them know that
a cohort was being

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.010
created at a time they'd chosen.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:33.630
So I figured, we already all
have logins to for R4DS Slack.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.390
What if we made our
own app in Shiny

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.040
and then connected
that to the Slack API?

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.470
Then the app could
talk to our Slack

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:47.330
and connect each user to
their identity on R4DS.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.750
I posted the idea on our
Slack and one of our users,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.660
Priyanka Gagneja,
volunteered to make

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.390
the initial app minus
the login, and then I

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.720
focused on the login part.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.120
I'd already done some
work with Yoni Sidi

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.400
on his slackteams
package, which

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.280
allows you to talk
to Slack through R.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.340
You can find that and
Yoni's whole slackverse

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:14.800
family of packages on GitHub
at yonicd slash slackteams.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.520
My goal was to take what I did
there with interactive Slack

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.210
authorization, make
it work in Shiny,

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:26.260
and then wrap that up into
an easy to use package.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.510
So this is what that looks like.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.550
If you aren't
already logged in, it

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.130
asks you to log in using Slack.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.730
From that initial screen, you're
taken to Slack's permission

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.490
screen to connect to
your Slack account,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.980
and then it returns
you to the Shiny app.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.710
Here we just use the connection
to identify the user,

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.330
but I have another
app where we use

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.280
it to make sure the
user has permission

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.760
to see certain messages.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.170
And I'm working on one that will
help users ask better questions

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.360
and then post those
questions as them.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.480
So it works.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.130
Now let's see how I did on the
easy to use part of the goal.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.170
You can install
this from GitHub at

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.910
R4DS slash shinyslack.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.410
This is one of the ways we
can launch a normal Shiny app.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.220
We define a UI, define
a server function,

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.750
and wrap them in Shiny's
shinyApp function.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.010
Now we'll use shinyslack to
add a Slack login to that app.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:23.210
We replace Shiny's shinyApp
with shinyslack's shinyslack

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:24.020
app.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.470
We also need to give it the code
that identifies our Slack team,

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.240
and that's it.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.980
Well, kind of.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.740
So you probably want to
deploy your app to something

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.070
like shinyapps.io, not
just run the app locally.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:40.310
And to do that, you'll need
your own Slack app that

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.410
has permission to
send people to apps

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.470
hosted on your
Shiny apps account.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.450
When I first started
working on this,

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:51.560
setting up the Slack side of
things was really complicated.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.910
I was kind of dreading
explaining this part.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.150
I mean, I just did
that thing showing you

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.090
how easy it is to set up
the Shiny side of this.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.820
And this part was
going to ruin that.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.130
But then something
magical happened.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:07.970
Slack added an option to create
a Slack app from something

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:12.050
called a manifest. So I added
a function over in slackteams,

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.960
create custom app,
to make that easy.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.490
There's a vignette
explaining it,

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:20.149
but basically you call create
custom app with a couple

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:22.580
arguments and copy
paste the result

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.830
into Slack's app creation form.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.800
And with that, you can wrap
your Shiny app with shinyslack

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.540
and integrate it
with your Slack team.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.920
If you'd like to learn more, stop
by the R4DS Slack at r4ds.io/join

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.590
We're putting
together a new club

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.960
where we'll walk through
side projects like this

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.880
so you can learn all
about how this works

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.330
under the hood over there.

